# CV project agent standards

These rules are mandatory for every proposal, edit, commit, and push in this repo.

## 0. DESIGN CLARITY ABOVE ALL (most important)

**Clarity beats everything else** — aesthetics, cleverness, density, decoration.

- Cleanness of the site is the #1 product goal
- Least amount of text that still communicates; no chaos, no clutter, no competing focal points
- One clear hierarchy per view; whitespace over boxes; restraint over ornament
- A person using a screen reader (or otherwise reading without sighted layout cues) must be able to understand **all** content without friction
- Prefer semantic HTML (`header`, `nav`, `main`, `h1`–`h3`, lists, `button`/`a` correctly), meaningful link text, labels, and logical reading order
- Strong contrast, readable type size, clear focus states — accessibility is part of clarity, not an afterthought
- If a design choice adds noise without aiding understanding, remove it

Cross-examine every change:

> Is this the clearest possible version?

If not, simplify before shipping.

## 1. AAA modular design = general software modularity

Before creating, changing, proposing, or pushing anything, cross-examine it:

> Is this following modular, production-quality software design?

Meaning for this repo:

- **Shared CSS is mandatory** — one design system / stylesheet modules reused across pages
- **Never** invent different one-off styles per subpage that drift from the system
- **Identical site chrome on every page** — same header markup and placement (Experience, Samples, Reel, Resume, Contact — top-right only). No per-page brand link, no left-nav on one page and right-nav on another. Fixed nav uses a field-matched fade so scrolled content (especially Resume) passes underneath without colliding with the links
- **Resume** = `resume.html` subpage first (full letter-page preview on-site — PDF page image, not browser PDF chrome), plus **Open** (native browser PDF at root `resume.pdf`, Brittany Chiang pattern) and **Download** on the same left-edge column. Source for the PDF: `assets/resume/resume-source.html`; regenerate `Artur-Kosma-Resume.png` and root `resume.pdf` when the PDF changes
- **Reel** = `reel.html` subpage with an on-page YouTube facade spanning the full content shell (same width rhythm as Samples; click-to-load iframe; no eager embed). Keep first paint light.
- **Subpage alignment** = every page shares the `.page` left edge with nav right edge; do not invent arbitrary mid-width caps that drift from Samples / Contact rhythms
- **Contact** = miniature identity lockup (circular avatar **left of** name + Senior Animation Engineer) + quiet email with envelope icon aligned under the avatar column (click to copy) + icon-only LinkedIn / Facebook / GitHub centered under the lockup (no uppercase label column; no horizontal rules; avatar is identity only)
- Structure / presentation / behavior stay separated and reusable
- Predictable file layout; easy to add a page or portfolio item without rewriting the base
- No copy-paste CSS/HTML duplication when a shared class or partial pattern would do
- Clean code above clever code

Mirror what people treat as “perfect clean design”: clarity, hierarchy, restraint, consistency, accessibility — not visual noise.

## 1b. Design direction reference — Brittany Chiang portfolio-clean

**North star:** [brittanychiang.com](https://brittanychiang.com) (portfolio-clean direction).

Use as the **clarity / structure / restraint** reference — not a pixel clone, and not a blind restyle unless the user asks.

Patterns to follow from that direction:

- Short copy; every sentence earns its place
- One accent color for interactive affordances (links, active states, CTAs)
- Dark, quiet chrome; content louder than decoration — prefer text nav over bordered button chrome
- Experience list = typography first (dates + role/company); avoid heavy timeline ornaments
- Clear section hierarchy (About / Experience / Projects style thinking) with calm spacing
- Accessibility-first: skip link, semantic landmarks, keyboard-friendly, reduced-motion respect
- Shared design system across the whole site (no per-page style drift)
- Prefer lists and scannable blocks over long prose walls
- Subtle polish (hover/focus) over flashy motion
- **Expandable lists** = native `<details>`; multiple items may stay open. No exclusive accordion, no auto-scroll / pin / page-pad logic
- Subtle page-in on route change; item expands use height slide (`::details-content` 0fr→1fr), not opacity flash; respect reduced-motion
- **Portfolio/Samples row hover = accent-only (option A):** title color → accent, chevron brightens; **no background wash / no full-row highlight**
- **Samples expand copy:** short blurb under the title stays visible; each expand row is **one video + text to the right**, then the next video+text row underneath — never two media side by side
- **Samples row layout:** collapsed = video thumb + title/blurb; no large page H1 (“Portfolio”/“Samples”) above the list
- **Experience layout:** identity on the shared page-left edge (name → role line → portrait); timeline rail aligned to the left edge of the top nav (EXPERIENCE…CONTACT), right edge to the page shell; timeline top aligns with portrait top; collapsed timeline stretches so its bottom meets the portrait bottom; company → role → game title each on their own line on desktop (role louder than project; chevron in a reserved right gutter)

When proposing visual or structural changes, check:

> Does this move us closer to Brittany Chiang–level portfolio clarity?

Current colors/layout may stay until the user requests a restyle pass; still use this reference for new work and future redesigns. **Palette baseline:** original forest field + gold accent (`tokens.css`).

## 1c. Uber-giga-lightweight (zero wait)

The site must feel **instant**. We will add large images/GIFs (especially in experience / portfolio / samples). Performance is a hard constraint on every code decision.

**Asset location:** media lives **in this GitHub repo** (not an external CDN). That makes lean loading even more important — do not force visitors to download the whole media library on first paint.

Cross-examine every proposal:

> Is this the most efficient solution that still preserves clarity and quality?

Required patterns:

- **Load on demand** — heavy media must not load until needed (expand open, in-viewport, click-to-play, etc.)
- Prefer static HTML/CSS first; add JS only when it earns its weight
- No eager giant assets on first paint; landing page stays tiny
- Keep a clear `assets/` (or similar) layout; reference paths consistently; avoid duplicating the same binary
- Images: modern formats when possible (WebP/AVIF), correct dimensions, `loading="lazy"` / deferred decode; never ship full-res when a smaller derivative works
- Motion: choose the **lightest format that does not compromise quality** (usually compressed MP4/WebM over huge GIFs; use GIF only when it is truly the better quality/fit)
- GIFs/video: do not auto-download the heavy file until the user opts in (poster/thumbnail first; play on demand)
- Avoid heavy frameworks, big animation libraries, unused CSS/JS, and duplicate assets
- Cache-friendly structure; minimal requests on initial load
- Measure mentally: first contentful paint and interaction should feel immediate on PC and iPad

### In-repo asset layout (modular)

```
assets/
  portfolio/<project-slug>/
    thumb.webp      # tiny list/card preview (always cheap)
    poster.webp     # still frame before play (on-demand section)
    media.mp4       # primary motion (preferred when quality holds)
    media.webm      # optional alt
    media.gif       # only if required / quality-better than video
  experience/<item-slug>/   # same pattern when needed
  samples/<item-slug>/      # same pattern when needed
  shared/                   # site-wide icons/logos sparingly
```

Rules for that tree:

- One folder per item (`slug`); no dumped flat megabytes in one directory
- Always ship a lightweight `thumb` (and `poster` for motion) before the heavy `media.*`
- Never reference full `media.*` on first paint of landing or closed accordion rows
- Keep naming consistent so new items are drop-in modules

If a feature needs heavy media and would hurt first load, redesign the loading strategy before shipping — do not “add it and optimize later.”

## 2. PC + mobile parity (PC primary)

- **Primary target:** desktop / PC layout and polish
- **Required:** iPad and mobile must show the **same high visual quality**, not a degraded fallback
- Design both from the start — never “fix later”
- Touch targets, type, spacing, nav, and controls must feel intentional on iPad
- Avoid hover-only UX, tiny hit areas, overflow, or desktop-only assumptions

## 3. Ask questions aggressively

- Whenever even **slightly** unsure — **ask**
- No limit on how many questions; more is better than guessing
- Prefer short, concrete questions (options when helpful)
- Do not invent portfolio facts, bio, jobs, or major visual direction when uncertain
- **Portfolio placeholders stay until the user replaces them** — do not delete or rewrite with fake “real” entries unless asked

## 4. Extensive visual QA before every push (hard gate)

UI changes require **super extensive** testing. Agent viewport testing is enough (user is not required to re-check on device), but the bar is high.

**Do not push UI until all of the below pass** at minimum:

### Viewports (test all)
- Desktop wide (~1440px)
- Desktop medium (~1280px)
- iPad landscape (~1024–1180px)
- iPad portrait (~768–834px)
- Mobile (~390px)

### Surfaces to verify on each viewport
- Landing page and every subpage touched
- Top nav / Experience + Samples — must look like intentional controls, **never** default blue underlined browser links
- Typography hierarchy, spacing rhythm, overflow, wrapping
- Expand/collapse (portfolio details): open + closed states
- Focus/keyboard affordances where relevant
- Contrast and readability of brand, body, muted text, accents

### Method
- Use browser + screenshots (or equivalent) in this environment
- Compare PC vs iPad/mobile captures side by side mentally against clarity + parity rules
- Call out any residual risk before push; if blocked, ask — do not push blind

**Push rule:** no UI push without completed extensive visual QA evidence (screenshots / recorded checks).
